  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "While Denis asks Senya how to handle his jealousy over Katya, Chef Barinov pursues Mikhail into the hotel lobby where Mikhail is greeting Frau Kraus. Listen closely to the Russian dialogue.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Давай по дороге обсудим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "А как мне реагировать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Я же её люблю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Это нормально ревновать, нет?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Конечно, это нормально.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "У них ревнуешь — плохо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "И как мне быть?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Возьми, подойди к нему.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Пойми, Витя, завтра открытие.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Наш отель должен удивить гостей.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ресторан наш не трогай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Как вы провели эту ночь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "О, вижу, что получили.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Он только что расстался с другом.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Давай по дороге обсудим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's discuss it on the way.' Denis brings out the contrast between 'come on' and 'according to' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "А как мне реагировать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And how should I react?' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'to react' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Я же её люблю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'After all, I love her.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'like' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Это нормально ревновать, нет?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Is it normal to be jealous, or not?' Denis anchors the line on 'this', while 'no' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Конечно, это нормально.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Of course it is normal.' In Russian, the idea of 'of course' flows naturally into 'normally' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "У них ревнуешь — плохо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'With them, if you are jealous, it is bad.' Notice how Russian expresses 'at' and 'badly' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Denis answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "И как мне быть?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what should I do?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'be'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Возьми, подойди к нему.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Go ahead and walk up to him.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'take' before landing on 'him'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Пойми, Витя, завтра открытие.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Understand, Vitya, the opening is tomorrow.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'understand' and 'proper name' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Наш отель должен удивить гостей.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Our hotel must surprise the guests.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'our', while 'guests' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "senya",
   "t": "Ресторан наш не трогай.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do not touch our restaurant.' In Russian, the idea of 'restaurant' flows naturally into 'touch' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Как вы провели эту ночь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'How did you spend this night?' Mikhail raises the pitch on 'night' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "О, вижу, что получили.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, I see that you received it.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'about', while 'received' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "mikhail",
   "t": "Он только что расстался с другом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He just broke up with his friend.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'he', while 'friend' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
